  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "**This is the easy version of the problem. Here, you only need to compute f(S)f(S)f(S) for the given binary string SSS.**\n\nThe beauty of a **binary** string TTT, denoted f(T)f(T)f(T), is defined as follows.\n\nConsider the following algorithm (TTT is 111-indexed).\n\n\nLet score=0\\text{score} = 0score=0.\nRepeat the following ∣T∣−1|T|-1∣T∣−1 times:\n\nChoose an index iii (1≤i≤∣T∣1 \\leq i \\leq |T|1≤i≤∣T∣), delete TiT_iTi​ from the string, and concatenate the remaining parts together.\n\nNote that this reduces the length of TTT by 111.\nAfter the deletion, let KKK denote the number of indices iii such that 1≤i&lt;∣T∣1 \\leq i \\lt |T|1≤i&lt;∣T∣ and Ti≠Ti+1T_i \\neq T_{i+1}Ti​=Ti+1​.\nAdd KKK to score\\text{score}score.\n\n\nThe beauty of TTT is then defined to be the maximum possible value of score\\text{score}score at the end of this algorithm.\n\nYou are given a binary string SSS of length NNN. Find f(S)f(S)f(S).\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains one integer NNN — the length of SSS.\nThe next line contains the binary string SSS.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line f(S)f(S)f(S).",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 2\\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "SSS is a binary string, i.e, contains only the characters 000 and 111.",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
